course: dictionary
lesson: 10697
title: Unscrew and what it means
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Unscrew is a verb, so it tells you what someone does. To unscrew something is to open it or take it off by turning it. «Put a crab in a screw-top jar and an octopus will feel it, test it and then unscrew the lid» shows the action: turn the lid and it comes off.
explain: The word has two parts. Un- often means undo or reverse, and screw means fix something by turning it. So unscrew means to undo a screw by turning it. Ask yourself: what would the opposite action look like?
explain: Unscrew can also mean taking out screws. In design and technology, you unscrew screws with a screwdriver. The context tells you which meaning is used: a lid is opened by turning, and a screw is taken out by turning.
fact: Unscrew is a verb, so it shows an action.
fact: «Unscrew» means to remove or open something by turning it.
fact: «Unscrew» can also mean to take out screws, often with a screwdriver.
fact: Un- at the start of a word often means undo or reverse, so unscrew is the opposite of screw.
fact: Common phrases are «unscrew the lid» and «unscrew the top».
fact: The camel in «a camel unscrews its hump» is make-believe, because cartoons can show things real animals cannot do.
exercise: In «then unscrew the lid», what does unscrew mean? | Shout loudly at the lid | Paint the lid a new colour | *Turn the lid to open it | Press the lid down hard
exercise: In design and technology, what do you use to unscrew screws? | A wooden spoon | A paintbrush | *A screwdriver | A kettle
exercise: Which word is a verb in «Octopuses can learn to unscrew jar lids»? | *unscrew | lids | jar | Octopuses
test: In the cartoon, «a camel unscrews its hump and pours out water», is this real? | Yes, but only at the zoo | Yes, camels do this in the desert | Yes, every camel does it each morning | *No, it is a cartoon trick
test: Which word is the past tense of unscrew? | unscrewing | unscrew | unscrews | *unscrewed
exam: Which phrase means to open a jar by turning its lid? | Lift the jar high | Bang the table | *Unscrew the lid | Push the lid down
